WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.640
King Kong may be spry for his age,&nbsp;
but still he's lived a long time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.960
It's been more than 90 years since his birth&nbsp;
in the mind of Merian C. Cooper to the release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:14.000
of Godzilla vs. Kong — and he's stayed busy all&nbsp;
that time, living a long and complicated life.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.240
Many hands brought King Kong to&nbsp;
life, but it all began with Merian C.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.640
Cooper. As outlandish as the movie's story of&nbsp;
lost islands and daring adventures may seem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.880
it's not too far from Cooper's own life.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.480
In Kong: The History of a Movie&nbsp;
Icon from Fay Wray to Peter Jackson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.880
author Ray Morton chronicles the director's&nbsp;
career in what Cooper called "natural dramas,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.040
documentaries filmed all over the world,&nbsp;
then reshot and recut into narrative form.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.120
Even the seemingly inexplicable presence&nbsp;
of a movie starlet on a documentary shoot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.600
came from real life. Screenwriter Ruth Rose&nbsp;
based the fictional filmmaker Carl Denham's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.240
monologue about how every critic and exhibitor&nbsp;
demands a love story on Cooper's own complaints.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.480
The discovery of the Komodo dragon, a huge,&nbsp;
primitive reptile that had long been thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.360
extinct, fired Cooper's imagination. He conceived&nbsp;
a new natural drama about a war between komodo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:04.160
dragons and a clan of gorillas, eventually&nbsp;
whittled down to just one great ape. When Cooper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.880
learned the dragons he'd shipped over from Komodo&nbsp;
died in captivity, he found his tragic ending.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.320
Cooper commissioned a script from mystery&nbsp;
writer Edgar Wallace about a big game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.680
hunter who meets a rowboat full of&nbsp;
escaped convicts on a lost island.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.680
James A. Creelman rewrote it, replacing&nbsp;
the hunter with a Cooper-esque filmmaker.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.720
But their studio, RKO, balked at the cost of&nbsp;
filming live animals in their natural habitats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.240
Fortunately, they were also filming a&nbsp;
never-finished movie called Creation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.760
featuring stop-motion dinosaurs&nbsp;
created by the great Willis O'Brien,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.000
and Cooper found the man&nbsp;
to bring his Kong to life.

00:01:35.347 --> 00:01:39.200
The original King Kong tells the story of Carl&nbsp;
Denham, a filmmaker who journeys to a mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.160
island to document the legends of a godlike ape,&nbsp;
with down-on-her-luck actress Ann Darrow in tow.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.400
Upon landing, they interrupt a sacrifice to&nbsp;
Kong, and Ann finds herself on the altar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.720
Denham's crew fights their way across the island&nbsp;
and its prehistoric beasts, finally bringing Kong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:57.280
back to New York. He escapes, of course, and drags&nbsp;
Ann up the newly built Empire State Building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:01.520
then the tallest building on earth, until&nbsp;
a fleet of fighter pilots shoots him down.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.280
Co-director Ernest Shoedshack was horrified&nbsp;
to discover he could see the ways Willis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.040
O'Brien's team disturbed Kong's hair&nbsp;
as it jumped around between frames.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.800
Morton wrote that he "thought the whole&nbsp;
project was sunk" until an RKO executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.720
enthusiastically responded, "Look!&nbsp;
Kong is angry — his fur is bristling!"

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.160
As much as Cooper and O'Brien, the&nbsp;
credit for Kong's success goes to Max&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.320
Steiner for his pulse-pounding music.&nbsp;
But RKO were too cheap to commission a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.680
score and only paid Steiner to&nbsp;
patch together existing music.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.680
Cooper paid him out of pocket for an&nbsp;
original composition — money well spent.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.360
King Kong was a monster hit. Even at the height&nbsp;
of the Great Depression, it raked in more than $2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.240
million — which may not sound like much after&nbsp;
90 years of inflation, but it adds up to over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.600
$40 million in 2021 dollars. RKO was going&nbsp;
to milk this cash cow for all it was worth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.000
and that meant rushing a sequel into production&nbsp;
— just nine months from pitch to release.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.560
Son of Kong is a lot better&nbsp;
than you'd expect under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:57.760
circumstances — it can't live up&nbsp;
to the original, but it's not bad.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.320
Cooper and Rose came up with a surprisingly&nbsp;
realistic take on the aftermath of Kong's rampage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.240
with Carl Denham confined to his apartment&nbsp;
avoiding lawsuits and court summons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.360
Then again, they also seem to expect&nbsp;
us to feel more sorry for Denham than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.560
the hundreds of people who died as an&nbsp;
all-but-direct result of his actions.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.520
“I brought Kong back here. He got loose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.200
did a lot of damage, got killed and&nbsp;
that's that. What can I do about it?"

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.040
"We can go to jail for ten years."

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:24.080
Denham flees the country, where he finds&nbsp;
a fugitive who tricks him into helping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.000
him escape by claiming there's a treasure&nbsp;
on Skull Island — a fanciful claim which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.840
improbably turns out to be true. With&nbsp;
the movie already half over, they're off.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.840
On Skull Island, they finally meet&nbsp;
and befriend the titular Son of Kong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.680
a smaller, lighter colored baby ape.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:40.800
"Well if it isn't a little Kong!"

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.200
"He's stuck in the quicksand! He can't get out!"

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.040
"Well I never knew old Kong had a son!"

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.800
Then the movie ends with the whole island&nbsp;
falling apart right out of nowhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.360
and Li'l Kong sacrifices himself to save Denham.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.400
RKO rushed out Son of Kong to beat any&nbsp;
knockoffs to market, but that didn't stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.200
them from coming out later. Mighty Joe Young at&nbsp;
least has pedigree, with Shoedshack directing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.760
a starring role for Armstrong, a Cooper/Rose&nbsp;
script, and animation by Willis O'Brien and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.320
his young protegé Ray Harryhausen, who'd soon&nbsp;
become Hollywood's go-to stop-motion man.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.240
Konga, meanwhile, was a cheapie knockoff&nbsp;
with next to no pedigree, about a chimp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.360
taking a growth serum that turns him first into a&nbsp;
killer gorilla and then into a Kong-size monster.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:23.360
You could say the same about the cash-ins&nbsp;
that followed news of the 1976 remake.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.800
Legendary producers the Shaw Brothers&nbsp;
took a break from kung fu to produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.880
a Hong Kong King Kong with The Mighty&nbsp;
Peking Man, also known as Goliathon.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.040
A collaboration between South Korea&nbsp;
and a small American studio resulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.360
in a notoriously thrown-together picture called&nbsp;
A*P*E . And if you don't believe it's a ripoff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:44.000
just know that they originally wanted to call&nbsp;
it The New King Kong. It at least has one thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.960
no official Kong movie has — we doubt the Eighth&nbsp;
Wonder of the World ever flipped anyone the bird.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:53.360
Beyond the movies, we have Hanna-Barbera's&nbsp;
plus-sized cartoon star Grape Ape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:58.880
DC Comics' Titano the Super-Ape, a chimp who goes&nbsp;
monster-size thanks to the powers of kryptonite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.200
and Rampage, the video game that was later&nbsp;
adapted into a movie starring Dwayne Johnson.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:07.520
And we can't forget Donkey Kong, the arcade&nbsp;
classic that introduced the world to Mario.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.960
Even Godzilla himself owes a debt to Kong. He&nbsp;
would have been an O'Brien-esque stop-motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:17.680
creation if money hadn't been tight, and the&nbsp;
original Godzilla recreates Kong's elevated train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.280
attack. So of course they had to meet eventually.&nbsp;
Toho's 30th anniversary was a perfect opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:28.000
— how better to celebrate than to pit the studio's&nbsp;
biggest star against his American equivalent?

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.560
King Kong vs. Godzilla was only the third&nbsp;
Godzilla movie, the first in almost a decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.400
and it ended up deciding the direction the&nbsp;
franchise would take for decades to come.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.840
The original movie had been a deadly&nbsp;
serious allegory for atomic war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.400
but kids loved the big lizard anyway, and this new&nbsp;
movie was designed to appeal to those young fans.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.680
"Ah, I bet Kong has no chance."

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.960
"I'll toss you. Heads for King Kong."

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.360
Thanks to Godzilla's creator Ishiro Honda, there&nbsp;
was still social commentary. But this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:00.000
that meant a goofy satire of TV advertising&nbsp;
with some of Japan's biggest comedy stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.640
in a story about a drug company that captures&nbsp;
Kong to be their mascot and stages a fight between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.600
him and Godzilla by carrying him to the arena&nbsp;
with giant balloons. Not everyone was happy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.160
according to the book Japan's Favorite Mon-star by&nbsp;
Steve Ryfle, there were multiple effects artists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.040
who were horrified at some of the goofy things&nbsp;
coordinator Eiji Tsubaraya expected them to do.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.520
As for Kong, he's literally big in Japan&nbsp;
— he grew from small enough to comfortably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:28.000
interact with the human characters to Godzilla&nbsp;
size. That evened the odds, but also made the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.032
obligatory building-climbing scene less dramatic,&nbsp;
since the building's now smaller than Kong.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:38.560
King Kong returned to stateside screens in&nbsp;
1976 thanks to Italian superproducer Dino&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:43.280
De Laurentiis and a script by Lorenzo&nbsp;
Semple Jr., creator of TV's Batman.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.480
After the runaway success of&nbsp;
Jaws, monsters were big business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:50.080
and both De Laurentiis and Universal&nbsp;
made their own deals with RKO for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.000
rights to the big ape, starting a&nbsp;
tug-of-war that lasted for years.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.840
When the rights situation was finally sorted&nbsp;
out, De Laurentiis released a King Kong for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.000
the '70s in which Carl Denham is replaced&nbsp;
with a scummy oil executive who wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.640
exploit Skull Island's mineral resources,&nbsp;
while Jeff Bridges plays a hippie scientist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:10.240
Instead of the Empire State Building,&nbsp;
Kong climbs the World Trade Center.

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:12.800
That's special effects legend&nbsp;
Rick Baker in the ape suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:16.560
but he wasn't too happy with the final product.&nbsp;
He may have been thinking of the scene when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.400
Kong bathes Jessica Lange's character in&nbsp;
what looks like a mini-golf water feature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:24.000
and then blows her dry, his cheeks&nbsp;
inflating like big, rubbery balloons.

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:28.800
"I was wrong when I called this&nbsp;
thing a farce. It's a tragedy."

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.760
The crew had some even odder effects&nbsp;
ideas to cut costs — at one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:38.400
they considered casting a little person as Kong so&nbsp;
they could build even more miniature miniatures.

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:44.160
The 1976 King Kong is often remembered as a&nbsp;
flop since it failed in its stated goal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.440
outperforming Jaws, but by any other standards,&nbsp;
it was a huge hit, making back triple its cost.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:53.280
Even knowing that, it's hard to imagine&nbsp;
anyone was clamoring for a sequel exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:57.040
ten years and two days later, but&nbsp;
that's when King Kong Lives debuted.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:01.120
How does King Kong live, exactly? Because&nbsp;
a university has kept him on life support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:04.880
all this time and revived him with a&nbsp;
ginormous artificial heart, apparently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:09.360
But he's still lost the will to live, so the&nbsp;
scientists bring him a newly discovered female&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:14.160
Kong to jolt him awake. By the end, there's&nbsp;
even a relatively little baby Kong too!

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.400
Dino De Laurentiis explained his rationale&nbsp;
to American Cinematographer, saying,

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:20.720
"Steve McQueen made a picture&nbsp;
in which he died at the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:25.040
but they made another picture with Steve McQueen.&nbsp;
Many stars die at the end of a picture and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:29.840
go on to the next picture. Kong is a star. We&nbsp;
are going to have a new story, a new Kong."

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:34.320
Screenwriter Ron Shuset discussed with Fangoria&nbsp;
the many other possibilities De Laurentiis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:39.040
considered, which makes the plot they finally&nbsp;
went with look downright realistic. He shared,

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:44.320
"They had King Kong in Russia and King Kong in&nbsp;
outer space. Really, this is not an exaggeration."

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:49.280
Lord of the Rings director Peter Jackson fell in&nbsp;
love with King Kong the moment he saw it on TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:53.920
as a small child. The 2005 film is actually&nbsp;
his second remake of the story — Morton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:58.720
book reveals Jackson had already recreated&nbsp;
it with claymation and a camcorder at age 12.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:01.840
Universal gave him the chance to mount&nbsp;
a more professional remake on the set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.320
of his first Hollywood movie, The Frighteners.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:08.720
At first, he found the task too daunting to&nbsp;
consider, but he changed his mind out of worry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:13.040
Universal would offer the job to someone else&nbsp;
who might do even worse with it. But the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:16.960
went on hold until he followed up Return of&nbsp;
the King with the return of another king.

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:21.520
As you'd expect from such a devoted fan,&nbsp;
Jackson's King Kong closely follows the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:25.120
keeping the 1933 setting and&nbsp;
casting Naomi Watts, Jack Black,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:30.000
and Adrien Brody in the roles originated by&nbsp;
Fay Wray, Robert Armstrong, and Bruce Cabot.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.680
Obviously stop-motion would never&nbsp;
fly in the digital world of 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:37.040
so Jackson created a motion-capture&nbsp;
Kong with the help of Andy Serkis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:40.640
who'd proven the technology's potential&nbsp;
as Gollum in Lord of the Rings.

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:44.880
He and Jackson created a more literal, realistic&nbsp;
gorilla, and whatever you think of that choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.360
creatively, you can't deny his crew put in the&nbsp;
effort. Serkis, who pulls double duty as one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:53.280
of the sailors in the movie, spent weeks&nbsp;
in Rwanda, studying gorillas in the wild.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:58.800
Legendary Pictures laid the groundwork for&nbsp;
Godzilla vs. Kong by introducing yet another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:03.120
version of the Eighth Wonder of the World in&nbsp;
Kong: Skull Island. Like the last remake, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.760
period piece, but it shifts the action forward&nbsp;
to the '70s, as a motley crew of Vietnam vets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.760
a war photographer, and a government agent&nbsp;
arrive on Skull Island to investigate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:15.760
and get more than they bargain for when they&nbsp;
meet Kong and he wrecks their helicopters.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:19.360
The ape god's prehistoric world hasn't&nbsp;
been this entrancing since the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:21.760
thanks to all kinds of weird creatures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:26.080
But some of the wildest creatures are the human&nbsp;
ones, like John C. Reilly's wonderfully unhinged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:29.680
performance as a fighter pilot who's been&nbsp;
stranded on the island since World War II.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:34.960
"Iwis won't speak their name,&nbsp;
but I call 'em skullcrawlers."

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:36.340
"Why?"

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:38.800
"Because it sounds neat."

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:42.800
Skull Island also gives Kong some of the&nbsp;
best action sequences of his whole career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:46.560
building up to one in which a Skullcrawler&nbsp;
tries to bite his arm off only for Kong to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:51.200
reach all the way down its throat and pull its&nbsp;
guts out. If this movie's anything to go by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:55.040
the king's in good hands at Legendary&nbsp;
Pictures for his return to the screen.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:57.360
Check out one of our newest&nbsp;
videos right here! Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:01.920
even more Looper videos about your favorite&nbsp;
movies and TV shows are coming soon. Subscribe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:11.600
to our YouTube channel and hit the&nbsp;
bell so you don't miss a single one.

